/target

# libFuzzer build output, saved corpora and crash reproducers. A corpus is a
# cache — regenerable, machine-specific, and megabytes of it. A crash artifact
# that matters gets turned into a unit test in `plan_state_fuzz.rs` instead, so
# it runs on the release gate rather than only when someone has nightly.
/fuzz/target/
/fuzz/corpus/
/fuzz/artifacts/
/fuzz/coverage/
/fuzz/Cargo.lock

# cargo-mutants scratch: a run's survivors are a finding to act on, not an
# artefact to keep.
mutants.out/
mutants.out.old/

# Coverage artifacts. `cargo llvm-cov show-env` writes these into the repo
# root, so they need ignoring or they get swept up by `git add -A`.
*.profraw
*.profdata
node_modules/
*.log

# Credentials and keys. Belt and braces: nothing here should ever be written
# into the repo, the DSN lives outside it, and the TLS test scripts generate
# their certs into a mktemp dir. This is the net under all of that.
.env
.env.*
*.dsn
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.p12
secrets/

.DS_Store

# TPC-DS corpus is TPC-licensed; fetched, never vendored.
examples/tpcds/corpus/

# NOT ignored: `*.proptest-regressions`.
#
# proptest writes the seed of every failing case it finds into one of these, and
# its own header says to check the file in — the seeds replay before any new
# case is generated, so a defect found once is pinned forever for four lines.
#
# 93c395e committed `mask_properties.proptest-regressions` and added an ignore
# rule in the same commit. Ignoring does not affect an already-tracked file, so
# that one survived by accident and the rule quietly applied to whatever came
# next: `analysis_properties.proptest-regressions`, written two releases later,
# held the seed for the `ROLLUP`-over-an-alias disclosure and existed only on
# the machine that found it.
